  "result": "The gate returned **OK** for `drv.interaction.overtake_pass` (3 witnesses, 5 atoms read), on the third of six allowed runs.\n\n**What the concept says.** It holds at the observation where b comes to be behind a, if b was ahead of a with their headings aligned at some observation in the window before. A second coming-behind does not count unless b has been ahead again in between.\n- \"Ahead\" and \"behind\" use two readings together: the sign of the distance from a to b measured along a's heading (from `kin_heading` and `kin_position`), and a negative `rel_longitudinal_overlap` (the bodies are not side by side).\n- `rel_same_direction`, with the `same_dir_cos` parameter, decides whether the headings are aligned.\n\nNone of it needed a narrowing; every part comes from the contract's own wording.\n\n**The three test scenes.** The window is 6 s and `same_dir_cos` is 9/10. b is stopped at (100, 0).\n- **holds:** a follows b, moves to the adjacent lane at 4 s, is alongside for 3 s and has b 3 m behind at 8 s.\n- **fails:** a stops alongside b and stays side by side.\n- **blind:** the holds scene with `kin.position` masked.\n\n**What the first two failures changed.**\n1. If Core had computed the overlap in the blind scene, it would also have been unknown, with its own reason, and the gate rejects any unknown reason other than the masked atom. So the blind scene's source now measures the overlap itself. The gate also rejected supplying it while body sizes were present, so that source no longer observes body sizes.\n2. With no body sizes, the gate then rejected the scene because each road user's area on the road couldn't be worked out. The blind source therefore doesn't observe which lane anyone is in either.\n\nThe record's elements needed no change.\n\nTwo commands I tried were outside the one allowed command and were denied without running: a `grep` that started with `cd`, and a `--help` call on the gate. Neither was needed in the end, and the denied gate call did not use a run.\n\nThe details are in `task_result.md` in the working directory.",
